      "The spelling is two symbols. &attempts reads 'the address of attempts' — it copies the box's address onto your paper. *p reads 'the value in the box whose address is on paper p' — follow the address, open the box, take the value. And because the paper leads to the real box, *p = 42 does not change the paper; it walks to the box and changes what is inside — attempts itself becomes 42. Watch exactly that happen in the output below.",
      "Why does C bother? Because you have already been using pointers without the name: an array's name behaves like the address of its first box, a string is an array of chars reached by address, and passing a big struct to a function by address avoids copying the whole thing. Functions in the previous lesson received arrays — they received ADDRESSES. Pointers are not a new topic; they are the name for what was already true.",
